      "content": "<h3>Disadvantages and when NOT to use it</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>No order</strong>: iteration depends on hashes and capacity, not on insertion. Use <code>LinkedHashMap</code> or <code>TreeMap</code> if order matters.</li>\n<li><strong>Memory overhead</strong>: a Node object per entry plus the table array; costly for millions of small primitives (consider specialised maps).</li>\n<li><strong>Resizing pauses</strong>: one put can suddenly cost O(n).</li>\n<li><strong>Bad keys break it</strong>: missing <code>hashCode</code>, or a key changed after insertion, makes entries unfindable.</li>\n<li><strong>Not thread-safe</strong>: use <code>ConcurrentHashMap</code> for concurrent writes.</li>\n<li><strong>No range queries</strong>: for \"all keys between A and B\" a <code>TreeMap</code> is the right tool.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>For experienced developers:</strong> iteration cost is O(size + capacity), and hash flooding can degrade buckets to O(log n) (tree) or O(n) (non-comparable keys).</p>"
